{"name":"Zombie","hex":"#dadead","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zombie","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person, usually undead, animated by unnatural forces (such as magic), with no soul or will of his or her own, typically being slow, obedient, and harmless unless directed to be harmful.","A dead person, reanimated through some fictional agency as a fungus, virus, or the like, that has an insatiable desire to eat living humans, typically depicted as aggressive, fast moving, and infectious."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zombie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zombie","blocks":[{"text":"A zombie is a legendary undead being created through the reanimation of a cadaver, a corporeal manifestation of the revenant type. In modern popular culture, zombies often appear in horror genre works. The term comes from Haitian folklore, in which a zombie is a dead body reanimated through magic by a bokor sorcerer. Modern media depictions of the reanimation of the dead often do not involve magic but rather science fictional methods such as fungi, radiation, gases, infection, diseases, plants, bacteria, viruses, etc."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Zombie_haiti_ill_artlibre_jnl.png/330px-Zombie_haiti_ill_artlibre_jnl.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zombie"},{"title":"Zombie (The Cranberries song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Zombie\" is a protest song by Irish alternative rock band the Cranberries. It was written by the lead singer, Dolores O'Riordan, about the young victims of a bombing in Warrington, England, during the Troubles in Northern Ireland. The song was released on 19 September 1994 by Island Records as the lead single from the Cranberries' second studio album, No Need to Argue (1994). Critics have described \"Zombie\" as \"a masterpiece of alternative rock\", with grunge-style distorted guitar and shouted vocals uncharacteristic of the band's other work."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/16/The_Cranberries_-_Zombie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zombie_(The_Cranberries_song)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Zombies_NightoftheLivingDead.jpg/500px-Zombies_NightoftheLivingDead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Zombies_NightoftheLivingDead.jpg/1280px-Zombies_NightoftheLivingDead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Zombies_NightoftheLivingDead.jpg/500px-Zombies_NightoftheLivingDead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZombies_NightoftheLivingDead.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Vol_de_Zombis_%28Hector_Hyppolite_painting%29.jpg/500px-Vol_de_Zombis_%28Hector_Hyppolite_painting%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Vol_de_Zombis_%28Hector_Hyppolite_painting%29.jpg/1280px-Vol_de_Zombis_%28Hector_Hyppolite_painting%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Vol_de_Zombis_%28Hector_Hyppolite_painting%29.jpg/500px-Vol_de_Zombis_%28Hector_Hyppolite_painting%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1946 painting by Haitian artist Hector Hyppolite, of a bokor (with Baron Samedi iconography), engaged in body snatching from a cemetery in order to create zombies to serve him","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVol_de_Zombis_(Hector_Hyppolite_painting).